# Postgres for the booth app and the worker. docker-compose exposes the
# local one on 5436 so it does not clash with other projects' databases.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://booth:booth@localhost:5436/booth

# Where originals, composites and uploaded templates live. Relative paths
# resolve from the repo root. On the controller this is a directory on
# the SSD that the night-of checklist copies to two places.
BOOTH_DATA_DIR=./data

# The attendant's way into /admin from their phone. Anybody on the booth
# Wi-Fi can reach the controller, and admin can delete sessions and see
# every photo, so this is not optional. Generate one with:
#   openssl rand -base64 18
ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me

# Which printer the worker drives: fake (prints nothing, counts down the
# paper and ink anyway) or cups (the SELPHY, over USB on Linux or by
# AirPrint on a Mac, set up once with
# `sudo ops/printer.sh`; check it with `pnpm print:test`). The queue is
# the name ops/printer.sh gave it; CUPS is on the controller itself.
# Unset, the CUPS URL is localhost:631, except on a Mac, where the
# worker goes through CUPS's local socket so launchd starts CUPS.
BOOTH_PRINTER=fake
# BOOTH_PRINTER_QUEUE=SELPHY
# BOOTH_CUPS_URL=http://localhost:631

# The gallery: the event's own site, where guests upload their phone
# photos too, so the day is one album. The worker POSTs each finished
# session's photo there with the token (packages/worker/src/gallery.ts
# describes the request); the kiosk's QR code links to the session's
# page there. For the wedding that is the wedding-planner app:
#   GALLERY_SYNC_URL=https://wedding.example/api/booth/photos
#   GALLERY_SESSION_URL=https://wedding.example/i/booth/{id}
# and GALLERY_SYNC_TOKEN is its BOOTH_SYNC_TOKEN. Leave all three unset
# and sessions stay on the booth: the print is the copy, and the QR
# points at a page that does not exist.
GALLERY_SYNC_URL=
GALLERY_SYNC_TOKEN=
GALLERY_SESSION_URL=
